1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,408
Why is the sky blue?

2
00:00:01,728 --> 00:00:04,864
We asked two versions
of the same small language model.

3
00:00:05,184 --> 00:00:08,747
Both answers come from the course
staff's saved Colab run.

4
00:00:08,983 --> 00:00:11,159
The original answers like a textbook.

5
00:00:11,479 --> 00:00:16,130
The fine-tuned one opens with a cheesy
greeting in the instructor's voice,

6
00:00:16,130 --> 00:00:17,261
and then explains.

7
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:18,908
Now check the physics.

8
00:00:19,228 --> 00:00:22,385
One blames refraction,
the other water molecules.

9
00:00:22,705 --> 00:00:26,375
Both are wrong: air molecules
scatter blue light more than red.

10
00:00:26,695 --> 00:00:30,151
The voice changed completely;
the facts did not get better.

11
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,032
Chat models learn their habits the same way;

12
00:00:33,032 --> 00:00:36,800
the lecture shows one that starts
with the words, Sure, here's.

13
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:43,947
This one kept 596 million weights frozen
and trained 1.15 million new ones.

14
00:00:44,267 --> 00:00:47,646
It took about 3 and a half
minutes on an A100,

15
00:00:47,646 --> 00:00:50,731
and fits in a file of about 4.6 megabytes.

16
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:53,036
So what did it learn, and what didn't it?

17
00:00:53,356 --> 00:00:54,786
Two tracks answer that.

18
00:00:55,106 --> 00:00:59,098
In track A,
the README, Gemini writes a persona dataset

19
00:00:59,098 --> 00:01:01,420
and Google tunes Gemini for you.

20
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:03,582
In track B, the course Colab,

21
00:01:03,582 --> 00:01:07,265
you fine-tune an open model yourself
and keep the weights.

22
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,895
The tracker, top left, shows the step;

23
00:01:09,895 --> 00:01:13,047
the gray tag,
top right, shows the lecture slides.

24
00:01:13,367 --> 00:01:16,375
When a tag says your turn,
pause and run the command.

25
00:01:18,883 --> 00:01:23,963
One honest limit:
we recorded this on October 31, 2026,

26
00:01:23,963 --> 00:01:27,566
with no service account key and no GPU.

27
00:01:27,886 --> 00:01:30,783
Every README command ran exactly as written;

28
00:01:30,783 --> 00:01:34,273
the ones that talk
to Google stop at the missing key,

29
00:01:34,273 --> 00:01:36,248
and we keep those real errors.

30
00:01:36,568 --> 00:01:40,473
Working outputs come from the README
and the saved Colab run.

31
00:01:43,317 --> 00:01:45,962
There are three ways to change
what a model says.

32
00:01:46,282 --> 00:01:49,120
A prompt changes the input
every time you ask.

33
00:01:49,440 --> 00:01:54,197
Retrieval, the RAG app from lecture 5,
adds looked-up text to the input.

34
00:01:54,517 --> 00:01:56,778
Both leave the model itself untouched.

35
00:01:57,017 --> 00:02:00,807
Fine-tuning turns the third dial:
it changes the weights,

36
00:02:00,807 --> 00:02:03,910
by showing the model
many examples of an input

37
00:02:03,910 --> 00:02:05,529
and the output you want.

38
00:02:05,849 --> 00:02:09,510
In this course
that usually means instruction fine-tuning:

39
00:02:09,510 --> 00:02:11,972
pairs of an instruction and its answer.

40
00:02:12,292 --> 00:02:15,001
A persona is a task-specific fine-tune.

41
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:18,194
Compare a model
that was never instruction-tuned.

42
00:02:18,514 --> 00:02:24,167
The notebook gives OPT 350m the words,
hello, my cheese name is.

43
00:02:24,487 --> 00:02:25,878
It simply keeps going:

44
00:02:25,878 --> 00:02:29,864
my cheese name is cheese,
I'm not sure if you're joking or not.

45
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:33,671
An instruction-tuned model
answers instead of continuing,

46
00:02:33,671 --> 00:02:36,052
often with a habit like, Sure, here's.

47
00:02:36,372 --> 00:02:39,785
Our fine-tuned Qwen answers
the way the instructor would.

48
00:02:40,105 --> 00:02:42,373
That's the lesson of the saved run:

49
00:02:42,373 --> 00:02:45,290
style is learnable;
facts are not guaranteed.

50
00:02:45,534 --> 00:02:50,230
A useful mental model:
fine-tuning moves the model's default voice;

51
00:02:50,230 --> 00:02:52,894
RAG changes the open book on its desk.

52
00:02:53,214 --> 00:02:56,990
In track A, Google trains the model
and you never see a weight.

53
00:02:57,310 --> 00:03:01,157
In track B,
you train a small adapter on a Colab GPU,

54
00:03:01,157 --> 00:03:02,899
and it is yours to save.

55
00:03:05,033 --> 00:03:10,537
Start in your workspace, the AC215 folder,
with Docker installed and running.

56
00:03:10,857 --> 00:03:15,486
The README only links the repository,
so cloning it is an extra step.

57
00:03:15,806 --> 00:03:18,345
Your prompt shows
your own user name and machine.

58
00:03:18,583 --> 00:03:21,676
Recorded at the commit on screen;
yours may be newer.

59
00:03:21,996 --> 00:03:26,421
Inside are two folders,
dataset-creator and gemini-finetuner,

60
00:03:26,421 --> 00:03:28,524
each with the same six files.

61
00:03:28,844 --> 00:03:31,837
The autotrain folder
is not part of the README,

62
00:03:31,837 --> 00:03:32,983
so leave it alone.

63
00:03:33,233 --> 00:03:35,580
Step 2 happens in the cloud console.

64
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:40,799
Create a service account called
llm-service-account with two roles:

65
00:03:40,799 --> 00:03:45,478
Storage Admin, and Agent Platform User,
formerly Vertex AI User.

66
00:03:45,798 --> 00:03:49,852
Under keys, add a JSON key,
which downloads to your laptop.

67
00:03:50,172 --> 00:03:53,308
If you kept the account from lecture 5,
reuse it.

68
00:03:53,550 --> 00:03:58,670
Step 3: make a folder called secrets next
to the repository, not inside it.

69
00:03:58,990 --> 00:04:04,629
Move the key in and name it exactly
llm-service-account.json;

70
00:04:04,629 --> 00:04:09,806
with hidden file extensions
it can end up as .json.json.

71
00:04:10,126 --> 00:04:12,003
Ours stays empty on purpose.

72
00:04:12,323 --> 00:04:13,667
Watch what that does.

73
00:04:13,916 --> 00:04:15,751
Why next to the repository?

74
00:04:16,071 --> 00:04:19,655
A key outside the clone
can never be committed by accident.

75
00:04:19,975 --> 00:04:24,417
The env.dev file looks two folders up
from wherever the script runs,

76
00:04:24,417 --> 00:04:27,356
so both container folders
reach the same key.

77
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:31,836
An extra step 4,
implied by the file's own comments:

78
00:04:31,836 --> 00:04:37,456
in env.dev, set your own project ID
and bucket name on lines 6 and 7.

79
00:04:37,776 --> 00:04:40,186
The scripts pass both into the containers.

80
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:45,389
The README also assumes the Agent Platform
and Storage APIs are enabled,

81
00:04:45,389 --> 00:04:47,110
and a bucket of your own.

82
00:04:47,430 --> 00:04:51,526
Bucket names are global,
and cheese-dataset belongs to the course.

83
00:04:51,846 --> 00:04:54,001
Pause here and finish all four steps.

84
00:04:57,150 --> 00:05:01,886
Step 1 of the dataset section:
go into the dataset-creator folder.

85
00:05:02,206 --> 00:05:04,937
Step 2: sh docker-shell.sh.

86
00:05:05,257 --> 00:05:07,326
The first build installs everything.

87
00:05:07,646 --> 00:05:10,686
Here it took 45 seconds
on a fast connection;

88
00:05:10,686 --> 00:05:12,105
expect a few minutes.

89
00:05:12,425 --> 00:05:15,497
If you see source:
not found, the fix is coming.

90
00:05:15,733 --> 00:05:17,077
What did the script do?

91
00:05:17,397 --> 00:05:22,803
It reads env.dev, builds the image,
and runs it with two folders mounted:

92
00:05:22,803 --> 00:05:27,765
this folder becomes /app,
and your secrets folder becomes /secrets.

93
00:05:28,085 --> 00:05:32,032
Three variables carry the key's path,
your project and your bucket.

94
00:05:32,352 --> 00:05:36,491
The --rm flag removes the container
when you exit.

95
00:05:36,733 --> 00:05:38,281
Read the banner kindly:

96
00:05:38,281 --> 00:05:41,647
it says activated
before the activation line runs,

97
00:05:41,647 --> 00:05:44,370
and the prompt never shows an app prefix.

98
00:05:44,690 --> 00:05:45,964
Both are harmless;

99
00:05:45,964 --> 00:05:49,267
an extra check shows
Python comes from /.venv,

100
00:05:49,267 --> 00:05:51,133
and your settings arrived.

101
00:05:51,453 --> 00:05:55,118
The part after app
and the at sign is the container ID;

102
00:05:55,118 --> 00:05:56,317
yours will differ.

103
00:05:56,567 --> 00:06:02,391
Step 3 is the README's tip:
ls -la /secrets, inside the container.

104
00:06:02,711 --> 00:06:06,892
With a key,
you'd see llm-service-account.json here.

105
00:06:07,212 --> 00:06:10,199
Ours shows only the folder itself,
on purpose.

106
00:06:10,433 --> 00:06:13,953
Step 4: python cli.py --help.

107
00:06:14,273 --> 00:06:17,238
Three flags: generate, prepare and upload.

108
00:06:17,558 --> 00:06:18,842
It works without a key,

109
00:06:18,842 --> 00:06:22,806
because the code only looks for credentials
when it first calls Google.

110
00:06:23,126 --> 00:06:24,876
Pause, and get to this prompt.

111
00:06:27,117 --> 00:06:30,189
Two traps,
shown as extras from a second terminal.

112
00:06:30,509 --> 00:06:36,624
On Debian, Ubuntu or WSL, sh is dash,
which has no source command:

113
00:06:36,624 --> 00:06:39,682
run bash docker-shell.sh instead.

114
00:06:40,002 --> 00:06:44,617
And from the repository root,
the script cannot find env.dev:

115
00:06:44,617 --> 00:06:46,509
cd into the folder first.

116
00:06:47,933 --> 00:06:52,776
One more extra:
docker images reports 1.64 gigabytes.

117
00:06:53,096 --> 00:06:55,878
The lockfile installs 133 packages,

118
00:06:55,878 --> 00:07:00,012
among them the RAG
lecture's chromadb and langchain,

119
00:07:00,012 --> 00:07:02,397
which this code never imports.

120
00:07:06,633 --> 00:07:10,833
The lecture's instruction dataset
has three parts per example:

121
00:07:10,833 --> 00:07:14,761
an instruction, optional context,
and the output you want.

122
00:07:15,081 --> 00:07:20,052
The tutorial drops the context:
each example is a question and an answer.

123
00:07:20,372 --> 00:07:21,972
Who writes a hundred of them?

124
00:07:22,292 --> 00:07:23,998
Another model, a teacher.

125
00:07:24,233 --> 00:07:29,545
Here the teacher is Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite,
called in the global location.

126
00:07:29,865 --> 00:07:33,538
It gets a system instruction
describing the persona,

127
00:07:33,538 --> 00:07:36,081
an input prompt asking for 20 pairs,

128
00:07:36,081 --> 00:07:39,472
and a JSON schema
that fixes the answer's shape:

129
00:07:39,472 --> 00:07:41,449
question and answer objects.

130
00:07:42,583 --> 00:07:46,466
Step 1: python cli.py --generate.

131
00:07:46,786 --> 00:07:48,866
With a key, it costs cents:

132
00:07:48,866 --> 00:07:52,332
the README estimates
$2 per 1,000 iterations.

133
00:07:52,652 --> 00:07:55,462
Without one,
every batch prints the same line:

134
00:07:55,462 --> 00:07:57,111
the key file was not found.

135
00:07:57,431 --> 00:08:00,716
And yet the exit status is zero,
which means success.

136
00:08:01,036 --> 00:08:02,316
Remember that zero.

137
00:08:02,566 --> 00:08:07,323
Step 2 explains the system prompt,
which lives in cli.py.

138
00:08:07,643 --> 00:08:12,796
It tells the teacher to open each answer
with one of the instructor's catchphrases,

139
00:08:12,796 --> 00:08:15,217
such as good question, or glad you ask.

140
00:08:15,537 --> 00:08:20,166
And it asks for enthusiastic exclamations,
in the instructor's character.

141
00:08:20,416 --> 00:08:24,512
Step 3 is the input prompt,
asking for 20 diverse pairs.

142
00:08:24,832 --> 00:08:30,421
Step 4: the number of iterations
is a constant on line 170, not a flag;

143
00:08:30,421 --> 00:08:32,704
edit it for a bigger dataset.

144
00:08:33,024 --> 00:08:35,351
Temperature 0.9 adds variety,

145
00:08:35,351 --> 00:08:40,406
and the response schema makes
Gemini return JSON in that shape.

146
00:08:40,650 --> 00:08:44,877
With a key, the files would hold
answers like the README's samples:

147
00:08:44,877 --> 00:08:48,031
Yello, this is the instructor,
your cheese monker.

148
00:08:48,351 --> 00:08:54,547
A small slip: the README's prompt
excerpt lists older openers than cli.py,

149
00:08:54,547 --> 00:08:59,487
but cli.py's own samples still open
with Yello and Welcome.

150
00:08:59,807 --> 00:09:00,980
Expect a mix.

151
00:09:01,216 --> 00:09:03,371
One question before we move on.

152
00:09:03,691 --> 00:09:08,384
The prompt asks the teacher for specifics,
such as optimal pH levels.

153
00:09:17,233 --> 00:09:18,982
It becomes a training target.

154
00:09:19,302 --> 00:09:23,420
The student learns to say it,
confidently, in the instructor's voice.

155
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:26,483
Synthetic data carries
the teacher's mistakes,

156
00:09:26,483 --> 00:09:28,390
so check what the teacher wrote.

157
00:09:29,732 --> 00:09:31,758
Now step 1 of the prepare section,

158
00:09:31,758 --> 00:09:34,617
as a student would type it right
after that run.

159
00:09:34,937 --> 00:09:38,116
Type python cli.py --prepare.

160
00:09:38,436 --> 00:09:42,105
Zero errors, a shape of zero by zero,
and then a crash.

161
00:09:42,425 --> 00:09:47,247
KeyError:
'question', from line 243 of cli.py.

162
00:09:47,482 --> 00:09:48,591
Trace it back.

163
00:09:48,911 --> 00:09:52,363
In generate,
each batch runs inside a try block,

164
00:09:52,363 --> 00:09:55,934
and the except
on line 200 catches any exception,

165
00:09:55,934 --> 00:09:57,829
prints it, and carries on.

166
00:09:58,149 --> 00:10:02,031
So no file was written,
and the command still exited with zero.

167
00:10:02,265 --> 00:10:05,135
Prepare found no files,
built an empty table,

168
00:10:05,135 --> 00:10:08,580
and failed the first time it read
the question column.

169
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:10,685
The error was there all along;

170
00:10:10,685 --> 00:10:13,422
the catch-all only moved
it one command later.

171
00:10:13,742 --> 00:10:16,004
Read the lines, not the exit code.

172
00:10:16,249 --> 00:10:18,298
The fix is the key itself:

173
00:10:18,298 --> 00:10:22,159
put it in the secrets folder
with the exact name,

174
00:10:22,159 --> 00:10:24,569
and check with ls -la /secrets.

175
00:10:24,815 --> 00:10:29,255
So we can watch a real run, an extra:
five hand-written files,

176
00:10:29,255 --> 00:10:33,050
tagged scripted,
in exactly the shape Gemini returns.

177
00:10:33,370 --> 00:10:37,346
They hold 20 pairs,
where Gemini would write about a hundred;

178
00:10:37,346 --> 00:10:39,236
your generate writes its own.

179
00:10:39,556 --> 00:10:43,077
Note the 1-byte CSV
the failed run left behind.

180
00:10:43,315 --> 00:10:44,595
Now prepare again.

181
00:10:44,915 --> 00:10:49,416
Five files processed, zero errors,
and a shape of 19 by 2.

182
00:10:49,736 --> 00:10:53,534
The last scripted pair repeats
the first question on purpose.

183
00:10:53,854 --> 00:10:57,502
drop_duplicates on line 233 removed it.

184
00:10:57,822 --> 00:11:00,041
Every answer opens with a catchphrase.

185
00:11:00,282 --> 00:11:06,383
The rows are saved as instruct-dataset.csv:
the lecture's term, in the file name.

186
00:11:06,703 --> 00:11:09,498
Pause, and run
generate and prepare yourself.

187
00:11:12,832 --> 00:11:15,136
Step 2 of prepare is about format.

188
00:11:15,456 --> 00:11:16,693
Follow one pair.

189
00:11:17,013 --> 00:11:18,912
It starts as a CSV row.

190
00:11:19,232 --> 00:11:25,214
Lines 241 to 247 glue it into one text
with human and bot labels,

191
00:11:25,214 --> 00:11:30,368
the text column the repository's autotrain
folder reads.

192
00:11:30,688 --> 00:11:32,373
Track A doesn't use that one.

193
00:11:32,615 --> 00:11:37,090
Lines 251 to 257 build
what Gemini tuning expects:

194
00:11:37,090 --> 00:11:39,148
a list called contents,

195
00:11:39,148 --> 00:11:44,711
with a user turn and a model turn,
each holding parts and text.

196
00:11:44,949 --> 00:11:47,850
An extra look at the real train.jsonl:

197
00:11:47,850 --> 00:11:53,653
the same nesting as the README's example,
but each example on a single line.

198
00:11:53,973 --> 00:11:57,130
That's the L in JSONL: JSON lines.

199
00:11:57,365 --> 00:12:02,747
Then train_test_split
holds out 10 percent with a fixed seed:

200
00:12:02,747 --> 00:12:10,336
17 examples in train.jsonl, 2 in test.jsonl,
which the tuning job uses for validation.

201
00:12:10,656 --> 00:12:13,035
The code's comment says Gemini

202
00:12:13,035 --> 00:12:17,397
accepts at most 256 validation examples,
hence the cap.

203
00:12:21,099 --> 00:12:24,940
Whatever the wrapping,
training is still next-word prediction,

204
00:12:24,940 --> 00:12:26,923
now on the instructor's answers.

205
00:12:28,599 --> 00:12:30,409
An extra from the laptop side:

206
00:12:30,409 --> 00:12:33,186
the bind mount left these files
in your clone.

207
00:12:33,506 --> 00:12:38,177
git status shows the data folder as ignored,
so it stays out of your commits.

208
00:12:38,415 --> 00:12:43,236
Next section, upload:
python cli.py --upload.

209
00:12:43,556 --> 00:12:47,387
This time it fails loudly,
with a DefaultCredentialsError:

210
00:12:47,387 --> 00:12:48,387
file not found.

211
00:12:48,698 --> 00:12:51,215
With a key, no output is documented.

212
00:12:51,535 --> 00:12:54,938
Look for one line per file
starting with Uploading file,

213
00:12:54,938 --> 00:12:57,551
five in all, then find them in your bucket.

214
00:12:57,799 --> 00:13:01,567
A small slip:
the README never says to create that bucket,

215
00:13:01,567 --> 00:13:03,452
and upload assumes it exists.

216
00:13:03,772 --> 00:13:04,924
Make it first.

217
00:13:05,244 --> 00:13:08,722
If you just changed env.dev,
restart the container.

218
00:13:09,042 --> 00:13:10,535
Then pause and upload.

219
00:13:13,882 --> 00:13:15,610
On to the fine-tune section.

220
00:13:15,930 --> 00:13:18,841
Type exit; the container removes itself,

221
00:13:18,841 --> 00:13:22,117
and an extra docker ps
-a shows nothing left.

222
00:13:22,437 --> 00:13:25,487
Step 1: go to the gemini-finetuner folder.

223
00:13:26,132 --> 00:13:28,550
An extra edit the README leaves out:

224
00:13:28,550 --> 00:13:32,916
lines 10 and 11 still point
at the course bucket, cheese-dataset.

225
00:13:33,236 --> 00:13:36,777
Replace only cheese-dataset
with your bucket name;

226
00:13:36,777 --> 00:13:41,876
keep the folder, llm-finetune-dataset-small,
where upload put the files.

227
00:13:42,196 --> 00:13:46,356
The folder is mounted into the container,
so there's no need to rebuild.

228
00:13:46,599 --> 00:13:49,778
Step 2: sh docker-shell.sh again.

229
00:13:50,098 --> 00:13:53,616
It's instant:
every layer up to uv sync is cached,

230
00:13:53,616 --> 00:13:56,923
because both folders
share the same Dockerfile,

231
00:13:56,923 --> 00:14:00,231
pyproject and lockfile,
as an extra diff shows.

232
00:14:01,465 --> 00:14:07,161
Step 3: python cli.py --help
shows two flags, train and chat.

233
00:14:07,481 --> 00:14:11,342
The train client creates
tuning jobs in us-central1.

234
00:14:11,662 --> 00:14:16,511
The chat client calls the tuned model
in the multi-region location us,

235
00:14:16,511 --> 00:14:17,828
at its own address.

236
00:14:18,065 --> 00:14:22,185
The train function wraps your bucket files
as tuning datasets,

237
00:14:22,185 --> 00:14:25,574
and asks for one epoch
and an adapter of size four.

238
00:14:25,894 --> 00:14:26,842
By our reading,

239
00:14:26,842 --> 00:14:31,078
that's Google's managed version
of the idea you'll build in part 2.

240
00:14:31,398 --> 00:14:33,020
One call starts the job.

241
00:14:33,265 --> 00:14:39,025
Step 1 of the fine-tune model section:
python cli.py --train.

242
00:14:39,345 --> 00:14:41,649
The experimental warning is harmless.

243
00:14:41,969 --> 00:14:44,764
Then the missing key: no job was created.

244
00:14:44,999 --> 00:14:49,901
With a key, look for Training job started,
a job name in us-central1,

245
00:14:49,901 --> 00:14:52,956
and after a minute, Tuned model info: None.

246
00:14:53,276 --> 00:14:56,152
Main calls train with wait_for_job=False,

247
00:14:56,152 --> 00:15:01,554
so the command hands in the job and leaves,
like a ticket at the dry cleaner.

248
00:15:01,874 --> 00:15:06,012
Before you pause, watch the cost table,
coming up in under a minute;

249
00:15:06,012 --> 00:15:08,081
then come back and start your job.

250
00:15:10,315 --> 00:15:13,914
Training needs far more
GPU memory than a laptop has;

251
00:15:13,914 --> 00:15:15,883
here that's Google's problem.

252
00:15:16,203 --> 00:15:20,229
Step 2: watch the job in the console
until it says succeeded,

253
00:15:20,229 --> 00:15:22,539
with a tuned model and an endpoint.

254
00:15:22,782 --> 00:15:24,339
A question to pause on.

255
00:15:33,182 --> 00:15:37,619
On Google's hardware in us-central1,
reading your bucket directly.

256
00:15:37,939 --> 00:15:41,993
Deleting the container loses nothing:
it only submitted the job.

257
00:15:43,932 --> 00:15:46,855
Step 1 of the cost section:
the README's table.

258
00:15:47,175 --> 00:15:50,921
At $0.008 per thousand tokens,

259
00:15:50,921 --> 00:15:56,790
16,444 documents over 3 epochs
come to $112.73;

260
00:15:56,790 --> 00:16:01,660
the table calls that way too much data.

261
00:16:01,980 --> 00:16:05,052
88 documents cost about 57 cents.

262
00:16:05,299 --> 00:16:10,658
A small slip: the table prices
Gemini 1.5 Flash for 3 epochs,

263
00:16:10,658 --> 00:16:14,963
while the code tunes
Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite for 1.

264
00:16:15,283 --> 00:16:19,613
Treat these numbers as stale
and check the pricing page the README links.

265
00:16:19,849 --> 00:16:22,281
Before you chat, one more extra edit.

266
00:16:22,601 --> 00:16:26,420
Line 107 still holds
the instructor's endpoint.

267
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:29,022
Copy yours from the console:

268
00:16:29,022 --> 00:16:33,097
a path ending
in locations/us/endpoints and an ID.

269
00:16:33,417 --> 00:16:37,249
Paste it there; otherwise chat points
at the instructor's endpoint,

270
00:16:37,249 --> 00:16:38,793
which your key can't reach.

271
00:16:39,032 --> 00:16:42,595
Step 2: python cli.py --chat.

272
00:16:42,915 --> 00:16:46,116
It prints its question,
how is cheese made in Greece,

273
00:16:46,116 --> 00:16:47,928
answered the instructor's way.

274
00:16:48,248 --> 00:16:52,493
Then it prints the serving location,
us, and the same missing key.

275
00:16:52,813 --> 00:16:55,331
Pause and run it once your endpoint is in.

276
00:16:57,565 --> 00:17:01,869
The README shows a working
endpoint's answer to an older question:

277
00:17:01,869 --> 00:17:03,304
the same Yello opener.

278
00:17:03,624 --> 00:17:05,075
Your answer will differ.

279
00:17:05,315 --> 00:17:07,918
Step 3, the hosted demo, is optional.

280
00:17:08,238 --> 00:17:12,664
Step 4: the console's training monitor
shows your job's loss curves

281
00:17:12,664 --> 00:17:14,382
and its data distribution.

282
00:17:14,632 --> 00:17:17,116
That's track A: a teacher wrote the data,

283
00:17:17,116 --> 00:17:20,328
Google ran the job,
and you have an endpoint to call.

284
00:17:20,648 --> 00:17:22,547
And you never saw a single weight.

285
00:17:22,867 --> 00:17:25,235
In part 2, the weights are in your hands.

286
00:17:27,282 --> 00:17:32,125
Part 2 happens in the course Colab,
Tutorial - Finetuning with QLoRA.

287
00:17:32,445 --> 00:17:36,135
Open it from the lecture's Ed lesson;
the link is on screen.

288
00:17:36,455 --> 00:17:40,594
First: File, Save a copy in Drive;
the original is view-only.

289
00:17:40,914 --> 00:17:44,754
Then: Runtime, Change runtime type,
and pick a GPU.

290
00:17:45,074 --> 00:17:46,695
A free T4 is enough.

291
00:17:48,432 --> 00:17:52,206
Section 3 pins
transformers, bitsandbytes and trl,

292
00:17:52,206 --> 00:17:53,716
and removes torchao,

293
00:17:53,716 --> 00:17:58,480
which, the notebook notes,
makes peft refuse to add LoRA layers.

294
00:17:58,715 --> 00:18:04,940
Section 4 needs a free Hugging Face token:
on huggingface.co, open Settings,

295
00:18:04,940 --> 00:18:11,494
then Access Tokens, make a read token,
and save it in Colab secrets as HF_TOKEN.

296
00:18:11,814 --> 00:18:16,064
Section 5 imports the libraries,
prints the versions,

297
00:18:16,064 --> 00:18:20,715
at the time of recording torch 2.11
and transformers 5.16,

298
00:18:20,715 --> 00:18:22,480
and mounts your Drive.

299
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:25,318
Section 6 caches both models there.

300
00:18:25,638 --> 00:18:27,174
Pause and run part A.

301
00:18:29,415 --> 00:18:31,036
Now the lecture's question.

302
00:18:31,356 --> 00:18:33,319
Why not simply train all the weights?

303
00:18:33,639 --> 00:18:37,653
Take a model with 10 billion
parameters in 16-bit floats:

304
00:18:37,653 --> 00:18:40,189
the weights alone take 20 gigabytes.

305
00:18:49,032 --> 00:18:52,595
The gradients, one per weight,
take another 20 gigabytes.

306
00:18:52,915 --> 00:18:56,441
And the Adam optimizer
keeps two running averages

307
00:18:56,441 --> 00:18:59,464
per weight in 32-bit floats: 80 gigabytes.

308
00:18:59,784 --> 00:19:05,682
That's 120 in total, the memory of five GPUs
with 24 gigabytes each,

309
00:19:05,682 --> 00:19:07,677
before any activations.

310
00:19:07,915 --> 00:19:10,141
LoRA trains only small adapters,

311
00:19:10,141 --> 00:19:13,479
so gradients and optimizer states
nearly vanish,

312
00:19:13,479 --> 00:19:17,792
but the frozen base still sits
in memory for the forward pass.

313
00:19:18,112 --> 00:19:20,843
The lecture puts that at about 40 gigabytes.

314
00:19:21,163 --> 00:19:26,475
QLoRA stores the base in 4 bits,
and the lecture's total drops to about 12.

315
00:19:26,795 --> 00:19:28,950
The next chapters are those three bars.

316
00:19:30,982 --> 00:19:34,214
Quantization means splitting a range
into buckets

317
00:19:34,214 --> 00:19:37,446
and storing only
which bucket a number fell into.

318
00:19:37,766 --> 00:19:39,660
The lecture builds LoRA first;

319
00:19:39,660 --> 00:19:42,566
we follow the notebook,
which quantizes first.

320
00:19:42,815 --> 00:19:51,391
Round 27, 55.3 and 83.78 to whole numbers:
27, 55 and 84.

321
00:19:51,711 --> 00:19:54,463
Round to tens: 30, 60 and 80.

322
00:19:54,783 --> 00:19:58,047
Fewer buckets need fewer bits,
and lose more detail.

323
00:19:58,282 --> 00:20:01,119
The lecture's worked example uses absmax.

324
00:20:01,439 --> 00:20:09,643
Ten values from 1.5 to 10.2
are multiplied by 127 over the largest,

325
00:20:09,643 --> 00:20:13,194
so 10.2 lands exactly on 127.

326
00:20:13,514 --> 00:20:21,151
Then each value rounds to an integer:
1.5 becomes 19, and 5.6 becomes 70.

327
00:20:21,399 --> 00:20:27,820
Divide back,
and 19 becomes 1.526, 70 becomes 5.622.

328
00:20:28,140 --> 00:20:31,440
Those small red gaps
are the dequantization error:

329
00:20:31,440 --> 00:20:33,751
quantization always loses a little.

330
00:20:34,071 --> 00:20:38,871
The slide shows 18 and 69;
rounding gives 19 and 70.

331
00:20:39,115 --> 00:20:41,964
One constant for a whole matrix is risky:

332
00:20:41,964 --> 00:20:45,579
one large weight squashes
everyone else's precision.

333
00:20:45,899 --> 00:20:51,105
So bitsandbytes keeps one absmax
constant per small block of 64 weights.

334
00:20:51,349 --> 00:20:53,269
Four bits give 16 levels.

335
00:20:53,589 --> 00:20:55,345
Weights are bell-shaped,

336
00:20:55,345 --> 00:20:59,003
so 4-bit NormalFloat, NF4, puts them
at quantiles:

337
00:20:59,003 --> 00:21:01,674
dense near zero, sparse in the tails.

338
00:21:01,994 --> 00:21:06,325
Each weight is stored as a 4-bit index;
two of them fit in one byte.

339
00:21:06,565 --> 00:21:08,454
Those block constants add up,

340
00:21:08,454 --> 00:21:11,450
so double quantization
quantizes them as well.

341
00:21:11,770 --> 00:21:13,172
The lecture skips it;

342
00:21:13,172 --> 00:21:16,443
the notebook turns it on,
right here, beside NF4.

343
00:21:16,682 --> 00:21:21,375
Stored in 4 bits, computed in 16:
for each matrix multiply,

344
00:21:21,375 --> 00:21:26,943
the layer's weights are expanded
to a 16-bit copy, used, then dropped.

345
00:21:27,263 --> 00:21:29,672
The lecture computes in BF16;

346
00:21:29,672 --> 00:21:34,325
this notebook in FP16,
as the training chapter explains.

347
00:21:34,565 --> 00:21:37,862
Section 1 of part B prints
the small OPT model:

348
00:21:37,862 --> 00:21:40,458
every linear layer is now Linear4bit,

349
00:21:40,458 --> 00:21:44,549
while the embeddings
and the output head stay as they were.

350
00:21:44,869 --> 00:21:47,935
Section 1 also tokenizes a short prompt,

351
00:21:47,935 --> 00:21:52,229
and section 2 generates
the continuation we met earlier.

352
00:21:52,549 --> 00:21:54,256
Pause, and run part B.

353
00:21:56,499 --> 00:21:58,459
Two misconceptions to drop:

354
00:21:58,459 --> 00:22:04,051
a 4-bit model does not do 4-bit arithmetic,
and the win is memory, not speed.

355
00:22:06,098 --> 00:22:13,287
Part C, step 1, loads the real student,
Qwen3 0.6B, with the same 4-bit recipe.

356
00:22:13,607 --> 00:22:17,229
Then prepare_model_for_kbit_training
freezes every weight

357
00:22:17,229 --> 00:22:22,247
and turns on gradient checkpointing,
a memory trick the training chapter shows.

358
00:22:22,481 --> 00:22:28,305
Section 2.1 counts the parameters:
375.8 million.

359
00:22:28,625 --> 00:22:33,297
But Qwen3 0.6B has 596 million.

360
00:22:42,131 --> 00:22:48,483
The 440 million weights in linear layers
are packed two 4-bit values to a byte,

361
00:22:48,483 --> 00:22:50,814
so they count as 220 million.

362
00:22:51,134 --> 00:22:57,064
The 155.6 million embedding weights
and the norms are not quantized.

363
00:22:57,384 --> 00:23:00,883
Add them up: 375.8 million.

364
00:23:01,203 --> 00:23:04,958
The configuration, in 2.2, confirms NF4.

365
00:23:05,198 --> 00:23:09,934
In 2.3 the base model continues a sentence,
and it drifts off topic.

366
00:23:10,254 --> 00:23:13,710
And 2.4 counts trainable parameters: zero.

367
00:23:14,030 --> 00:23:15,438
Everything is frozen.

368
00:23:16,481 --> 00:23:18,977
The notebook's step 3 prepares the data.

369
00:23:19,297 --> 00:23:23,884
It downloads a 23 megabyte file
and samples 1,000 pairs.

370
00:23:24,204 --> 00:23:27,830
Look at a row:
contents, a user turn, a model turn.

371
00:23:28,150 --> 00:23:32,524
It's exactly the format of track A:
the brick from part 1 arrives here.

372
00:23:32,765 --> 00:23:37,200
Section 3.5 lets the tokenizer
write Qwen's chat template:

373
00:23:37,200 --> 00:23:40,487
<|im_start|>user, the question, <|im_end|>,

374
00:23:40,487 --> 00:23:45,074
<|im_start|>assistant,
an empty think block, and the answer.

375
00:23:45,394 --> 00:23:49,514
The empty think block is how
Qwen3 answers without thinking,

376
00:23:49,514 --> 00:23:51,986
and chat uses the same prefix later.

377
00:23:52,231 --> 00:23:55,704
Section 3.6 splits 900 for training

378
00:23:55,704 --> 00:24:00,466
and 100 for testing,
and 3.7 checks one example.

379
00:24:00,786 --> 00:24:02,855
Then the ribbon becomes integers,

380
00:24:02,855 --> 00:24:06,866
and the loss covers every token,
question and template included.

381
00:24:07,186 --> 00:24:09,042
It's still next-word prediction.

382
00:24:09,362 --> 00:24:11,495
Pause, and run steps 1 to 3.

383
00:24:14,832 --> 00:24:18,035
Parameter-efficient
fine-tuning trains a small part

384
00:24:18,035 --> 00:24:19,355
and freezes the rest.

385
00:24:19,675 --> 00:24:23,127
The lecture sorts the methods
into three families:

386
00:24:23,127 --> 00:24:26,096
additive, selective and reparameterization.

387
00:24:26,416 --> 00:24:28,741
LoRA is a reparameterization.

388
00:24:28,982 --> 00:24:30,027
Start small.

389
00:24:30,347 --> 00:24:33,377
A 5 by 5 matrix has 25 numbers.

390
00:24:33,697 --> 00:24:39,401
A 5 by 1 column times a 1
by 5 row also makes a 5 by 5 matrix,

391
00:24:39,401 --> 00:24:41,334
from only 10 numbers.

392
00:24:41,654 --> 00:24:44,086
That's rank 1; rank 2 needs 20.

393
00:24:44,406 --> 00:24:46,497
At this size it's barely a saving.

394
00:24:46,732 --> 00:24:48,524
At real size it's different.

395
00:24:48,844 --> 00:24:53,798
Qwen's query projection
is a 2,048 by 1,024 matrix:

396
00:24:53,798 --> 00:24:56,033
over 2 million numbers.

397
00:24:56,353 --> 00:25:05,761
A rank 8 pair of strips,
2,048 by 8 and 8 by 1,024, holds 24,576.

398
00:25:07,999 --> 00:25:10,986
So LoRA decomposes the update,
not the weights.

399
00:25:11,306 --> 00:25:16,874
The new layer is the frozen W₀,
plus α/r, times B times A.

400
00:25:17,194 --> 00:25:19,007
Only B and A are trained.

401
00:25:19,249 --> 00:25:21,681
B starts at zero and A at random.

402
00:25:22,001 --> 00:25:25,394
The lecture draws
A from a normal distribution;

403
00:25:25,394 --> 00:25:27,633
the library uses a uniform one.

404
00:25:27,953 --> 00:25:31,601
What matters is that B times A starts
at exactly zero,

405
00:25:31,601 --> 00:25:34,844
so at step zero the model
is the pretrained one.

406
00:25:35,164 --> 00:25:36,998
Both branches' outputs add.

407
00:25:37,232 --> 00:25:42,562
Now the notebook's step 4,
the LoRA config: alpha 16 and rank 8,

408
00:25:42,562 --> 00:25:48,475
so the update is scaled by 2,
plus a dropout of 0.1 for regularization.

409
00:25:48,795 --> 00:25:49,947
Pause and run it.

410
00:25:52,182 --> 00:25:53,249
Which layers?

411
00:25:53,569 --> 00:25:55,958
The config names no target_modules.

412
00:25:56,278 --> 00:26:02,179
So for Qwen3, the PEFT library's default
adapts the query and value projections:

413
00:26:02,179 --> 00:26:05,942
two of the seven matrices
in each of the 28 blocks.

414
00:26:06,262 --> 00:26:13,793
That's 40,960 per block, 1,146,880 in all.

415
00:26:14,032 --> 00:26:17,662
The lecture's table treats a whole model
as one square layer;

416
00:26:17,662 --> 00:26:19,984
the real count depends on these shapes.

417
00:26:20,304 --> 00:26:21,776
Now, try it yourself.

418
00:26:30,615 --> 00:26:36,439
Rank 16 doubles it, to 2,293,760.

419
00:26:36,759 --> 00:26:42,903
All seven projections
at rank 8 give 5,046,272.

420
00:26:43,223 --> 00:26:46,770
More rank buys capacity
at more memory and time;

421
00:26:46,770 --> 00:26:50,540
for a persona,
rank 8 on two projections is plenty.

422
00:26:50,860 --> 00:26:54,103
To try it, reload the model in 1.3 first.

423
00:26:55,448 --> 00:26:59,437
Back to the memory column,
at Qwen's size, by our own estimate.

424
00:26:59,757 --> 00:27:04,351
Full fine-tuning would need
about 7 gigabytes before activations;

425
00:27:04,351 --> 00:27:06,541
this notebook's QLoRA, under 1.

426
00:27:06,861 --> 00:27:12,003
At 0.6 billion parameters,
even full fine-tuning would fit a T4.

427
00:27:12,323 --> 00:27:13,383
The recipe matters

428
00:27:13,383 --> 00:27:16,739
because the same notebook scales
to models that wouldn't.

429
00:27:17,981 --> 00:27:20,589
The rest of the memory goes to activations,

430
00:27:20,589 --> 00:27:23,661
the values each layer computes
on the way forward,

431
00:27:23,661 --> 00:27:25,320
kept for the backward pass.

432
00:27:25,640 --> 00:27:30,584
Gradient checkpointing keeps only a few
and recomputes the rest when needed:

433
00:27:30,584 --> 00:27:32,275
compute traded for memory.

434
00:27:32,515 --> 00:27:35,374
Section 4.2, the training arguments.

435
00:27:35,694 --> 00:27:40,175
Sequences are capped at 512 tokens
and grouped by length:

436
00:27:40,175 --> 00:27:42,007
2 epochs, batches of 4,

437
00:27:42,007 --> 00:27:45,912
and a learning rate
of 2 times 10 to the minus 4.

438
00:27:46,232 --> 00:27:49,262
The optimizer is a paged 32-bit Adam.

439
00:27:49,582 --> 00:27:54,574
Paging moves optimizer state to CPU memory
when the GPU fills up.

440
00:27:54,894 --> 00:27:56,387
Ours never needs to.

441
00:27:58,631 --> 00:28:01,703
Then fp16 on and bf16 off.

442
00:28:02,023 --> 00:28:06,234
BF16 keeps the range of float 32
with less precision,

443
00:28:06,234 --> 00:28:08,935
and the lecture uses it for QLoRA.

444
00:28:09,255 --> 00:28:14,802
The T4 has no fast BF16,
so this notebook computes in FP16.

445
00:28:15,048 --> 00:28:17,181
4.3 builds the trainer.

446
00:28:17,501 --> 00:28:23,280
The trainer turns the adapters into BF16,
which FP16 training cannot handle,

447
00:28:23,280 --> 00:28:26,397
so this cell casts them back to float 32.

448
00:28:26,717 --> 00:28:36,040
And the count confirms our arithmetic:
1,146,880 trainable parameters, 0.3 percent.

449
00:28:36,281 --> 00:28:40,367
In the backward pass,
the gradient flows down through W₀,

450
00:28:40,367 --> 00:28:42,446
and only B and A are updated.

451
00:28:42,766 --> 00:28:48,100
W₀ is used on the way down;
it just gets no gradient and no update.

452
00:28:48,348 --> 00:28:53,972
4.4, train:
900 examples in batches of 4 for 2 epochs

453
00:28:53,972 --> 00:29:00,444
is 450 steps, 3 minutes and 36 seconds
on the saved A100 run.

454
00:29:00,764 --> 00:29:03,537
The notebook says 6 to 7 minutes on a T4.

455
00:29:03,857 --> 00:29:07,569
The loss falls from 2.0 to 1.35.

456
00:29:07,889 --> 00:29:12,113
And 4.5, TensorBoard, shows these curves
in your own run.

457
00:29:12,433 --> 00:29:13,585
Pause and train.

458
00:29:16,932 --> 00:29:17,932
Did it work?

459
00:29:18,233 --> 00:29:20,367
Section 5.1 measures it.

460
00:29:20,687 --> 00:29:24,336
Perplexity is the exponential
of the average token loss:

461
00:29:24,336 --> 00:29:29,028
roughly how many equally likely next tokens
the model is choosing among.

462
00:29:29,815 --> 00:29:34,079
On 100 test examples, 30,681 tokens,

463
00:29:34,079 --> 00:29:41,186
the base model has
a loss of 2.65 and a perplexity of 14.15.

464
00:29:41,506 --> 00:29:45,815
The fine-tuned model:
1.39, and a perplexity of 4.

465
00:29:46,065 --> 00:29:49,926
The base row is the same model
inside disable_adapter().

466
00:29:50,246 --> 00:29:53,492
Switch the adapter off
and the original model,

467
00:29:53,492 --> 00:29:56,668
in 4 bits, is back,
because W₀ never changed.

468
00:29:56,988 --> 00:30:00,517
That's the lecture's point
about catastrophic forgetting,

469
00:30:00,517 --> 00:30:05,905
losing old skills while learning new ones:
with LoRA, the old model is one switch away.

470
00:30:06,149 --> 00:30:09,712
In the saved run,
every base answer starts with the word The.

471
00:30:10,032 --> 00:30:15,088
The fine-tuned ones start with welcome
or Yello, the openers of its training data.

472
00:30:15,408 --> 00:30:19,653
Those bars are an illustration;
the measured numbers sit beside them.

473
00:30:19,899 --> 00:30:21,392
What does the drop measure?

474
00:30:21,712 --> 00:30:25,446
The loss counts every token,
questions and template included.

475
00:30:25,766 --> 00:30:28,769
It says this style is what the model
now expects,

476
00:30:28,769 --> 00:30:31,099
not that its answers are more correct.

477
00:30:31,349 --> 00:30:33,376
5.2 compares answers.

478
00:30:33,696 --> 00:30:37,495
Both cheese answers now open
with welcome welcome welcome,

479
00:30:37,495 --> 00:30:40,181
cheese lovers, in the instructor's voice.

480
00:30:40,501 --> 00:30:43,829
And the sky:
the voice transferred, the physics did not.

481
00:30:44,149 --> 00:30:46,047
Pause, and run section 5.

482
00:30:48,282 --> 00:30:49,477
Two kind notes.

483
00:30:49,797 --> 00:30:53,225
In this comparison the base
is a fresh 16-bit model,

484
00:30:53,225 --> 00:30:57,050
not the 4-bit one from the table,
so the baselines differ.

485
00:30:57,370 --> 00:31:02,311
And the lecture shows LoRA matching
full fine-tuning on a published benchmark;

486
00:31:02,311 --> 00:31:05,669
this notebook measures style fit,
not answer quality.

487
00:31:07,515 --> 00:31:09,648
The notebook's step 6 saves your work.

488
00:31:09,968 --> 00:31:17,542
First the adapters: 1,146,880 numbers,
about 4.6 megabytes in float 32,

489
00:31:17,542 --> 00:31:21,702
saved to your Drive with the tokenizer.

490
00:31:26,148 --> 00:31:27,172
Then the merge.

491
00:31:27,492 --> 00:31:32,847
The notebook loads a fresh 16-bit copy
of the base and adds B times A into it.

492
00:31:33,167 --> 00:31:38,393
Not into the 4-bit weights: there,
the update would round back onto 16 levels.

493
00:31:38,713 --> 00:31:41,785
The merged model is about 1.2 gigabytes.

494
00:31:42,105 --> 00:31:45,025
If the merge raises a torchao ImportError,

495
00:31:45,025 --> 00:31:48,292
rerun the install cell
and restart the session.

496
00:31:50,532 --> 00:31:56,292
Section 6.3 can push the merged model
to a private Hugging Face repository.

497
00:31:56,612 --> 00:31:58,434
So you can ship three things:

498
00:31:58,434 --> 00:32:02,518
an adapter that needs its base,
a merged model that stands alone,

499
00:32:02,518 --> 00:32:05,785
or Google's endpoint,
with no weights in your hands.

500
00:32:06,032 --> 00:32:07,032
Teardown.

501
00:32:07,291 --> 00:32:12,005
Exit the container
and remove both images with docker image rm.

502
00:32:12,325 --> 00:32:14,864
Delete the dataset folder in your bucket.

503
00:32:15,184 --> 00:32:16,425
Keep the tuned model

504
00:32:16,425 --> 00:32:19,280
and endpoint for lecture 10,
then delete them.

505
00:32:19,600 --> 00:32:24,784
Delete unused service account keys,
and disconnect and delete the Colab runtime.

506
00:32:25,104 --> 00:32:26,469
Pause, and tear down.

507
00:32:28,715 --> 00:32:29,952
Here's where we are.

508
00:32:30,272 --> 00:32:32,640
Prompts and retrieval change the input;

509
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:36,950
fine-tuning changes the weights,
and here it taught a voice, not facts.

510
00:32:37,270 --> 00:32:41,344
In track A, a teacher wrote the data
and Google tuned the model.

511
00:32:41,664 --> 00:32:47,670
In track B, QLoRA froze a 4-bit base
and trained two thin matrices:

512
00:32:47,670 --> 00:32:50,539
120, then 40, then 12 gigabytes.

513
00:32:54,382 --> 00:32:56,387
Five questions to test yourself.

514
00:32:56,707 --> 00:32:59,331
What did this fine-tune change,
and what not?

515
00:32:59,651 --> 00:33:02,489
Why did generate exit zero without a key?

516
00:33:02,809 --> 00:33:05,817
Which shapes does one pair take
on its way to the template?

517
00:33:06,137 --> 00:33:08,718
Why does train print Tuned model info: None?

518
00:33:09,038 --> 00:33:14,030
And where does 1,146,880 come from?

519
00:33:17,265 --> 00:33:19,129
Next, lecture 9 tests code,

520
00:33:19,129 --> 00:33:22,236
and in lecture 10 an app
calls your endpoint.

521
00:33:22,556 --> 00:33:26,396
The study guide
and the exercises are in MLOps_Learning.

